From 8d00c582ecec4b7ac2da1f5fc3cf4393c67192cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PowerVLC <powervlc@localhost>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 14:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] bdj: run without a Security Manager on Java 24 and later

JEP 486 permanently disabled the Security Manager in Java 24:
System.setSecurityManager() always throws UnsupportedOperationException and
the private System.implSetSecurityManager() entry point used by libbluray's
Java 18 workaround no longer exists. This makes BD-J initialization fatal on
a machine whose only selected JVM is modern. The player then retries the disc
and looks as though it is loading forever.

Do not attempt an operation which the runtime cannot perform. The VFS no
longer relies on BDJSecurityManager to materialise files from an ISO: the
open path calls BDJLoader.accessFile() directly since the preceding PowerVLC
patch. Java 21 was already observed running through that path with
System.getSecurityManager() returning null, so Java 24 merely makes explicit
the effective mode already supported there.

Keep the existing implementation unchanged on Java 23 and earlier, including
the reflection workaround for Java 18 through 23 and the real manager on old
JVMs.
---
 src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/Libbluray.java | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/Libbluray.java b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/Libbluray.java
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/Libbluray.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/Libbluray.java
@@ -133,11 +133,25 @@ public class Libbluray {
     }
 
     private static void setSecurityManager(SecurityManager sm) throws Exception {
+        int javaMajor = getJavaMajor();
+
+        /* JEP 486 permanently disabled the Security Manager in Java 24.
+         * System.setSecurityManager() can no longer succeed and the private
+         * implSetSecurityManager() used by the Java 18 workaround was removed.
+         * BD-ROM file materialisation does not depend on the manager anymore,
+         * so continue without one on these runtimes. */
+        if (javaMajor >= 24) {
+            if (sm != null) {
+                System.err.println("Detected Java >= 24, continuing without a SecurityManager");
+            }
+            return;
+        }
+
         try {
             System.setSecurityManager(sm);
             return;
         } catch (Exception ex) {
-            if (getJavaMajor() < 18)
+            if (javaMajor < 18)
                 throw ex;
 
             /* Workaround for Java 18 security manager issues.
